#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143890} :activity/id #uuid "01f57b72-3b17-525a-9e25-49d9674b599f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049347 :topic/id #uuid "edd090be-d7a1-5d9f-adb3-93ff0e1be630" :topic/math-academy-id 2436} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143890 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186144002 :tutorial/content "When we divide a fraction by a whole number, say ${\\color{red}{2}},$ the idea is to \n\n- split the fraction into ${\\color{red}{2}}$ equal parts, and\n\n- determine the fraction that makes up each part.\n\nFor example, suppose we wish to find the value of \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{5} \\div 2.\n$$\n\nLet's start by drawing the fraction model for $\\dfrac45\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/d1/d159ff7e61ae308f27dd561cc8b219f0fc5b5fa46135ebff70ed15558186280b.png)\n\nWe need to divide this fraction by ${\\color{red}{2}}.$ To do this, we draw a horizontal line through our fraction that splits it into $\\color{red}2$ *equal parts*.\n\n![](images/87/87c0a9dad9ab63de7858e8857e412b4d6ce849dfc0b02a172a5debe420c5baf6.png)\n\nWe only need to determine the fraction that makes up one of the parts. Therefore, we can focus on only one row (i.e., the bottom row).\n\nNow, we remove all the shaded parts not in the bottom row. \n\n![](images/54/54a3760d69e7622afa800e4fe107cdafc7069a1fd31138a9ad6c63e8cf6f8f1f.png)\n\nFrom the picture on the right, we obtain that the shaded part represents $\\dfrac{4}{10}$ of the whole. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{5} \\div 2 = \\dfrac{4}{10} \\, .\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "411e9b60-5c7b-58a3-ba5a-8abc08a8f51a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2029 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "467f66f4-545a-520d-bca9-01a2adeee469" :step/math-academy-id 7605 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143891}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143891 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142942 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142717 :question/id #uuid "ea19